<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-21">21 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Vladimir Lenin dies at Gorki</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The founder of the Soviet state died aged 53 after a series of strokes. His body was embalmed and displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a>, and Stalin outmanoeuvred Trotsky and others to succeed him.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-25">25 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">First Winter Olympics open at Chamonix</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">An 'International Winter Sports Week' opened at Chamonix in the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> with athletes from 16 nations. The International Olympic Committee later recognised it as the first Winter Olympic Games.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-5">5 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">BBC broadcasts the Greenwich time signal for the first time</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The six short 'pips', generated by the Royal Observatory at Greenwich, were broadcast to mark the hour. The signal is still heard on BBC radio today.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-1">1 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Munich court sentences Hitler to five years for the Beer Hall Putsch</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> received five years' detention for his failed coup of November 1923. He served barely nine months at Landsberg, where he dictated Mein Kampf.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-2">2 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Coolidge signs the Indian Citizenship Act</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The law granted US citizenship to all Native Americans born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Some states still kept Native people from voting for decades afterwards.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/june-revolution-albania" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/june-revolution-albania.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolution</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/june-revolution-albania">June Revolution</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The June Revolution was an uprising in Albania in June 1924 that drove Ahmet Zogu from power and brought the Orthodox bishop and reformer Fan Noli to the head of the government.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda-convention">Klaipėda Convention</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Klaipėda Convention was an international agreement signed in Paris in May 1924 between Lithuania and Britain, France, Italy and Japan.</p></div></li></ol></section>
